  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in [0,12),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-12,12).$ What is the value of $f(-4)?$\n\n$f(-4)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,12),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,12), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-12,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-12,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-6(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-(-4)^{2}-6(-4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
